UNPUBLISH: use action "unpublish" when the user asks to take the site offline, cancel the publish, stop sharing, or disable its online link. The public link will stop working, so if the user was not explicit, confirm first. If the directory was never published or is already offline, report the returned status instead of claiming success. Deploy-only options are ignored for unpublish. This only cancels WEB publishes: a mini program has no share link, is released from its application panel, and is withdrawn from there too. Calling action "unpublish" for a mini-program directory is blocked and returns `{"type":"sites_unpublish_miniprogram_not_supported"}` with a ready-to-use `userMessage` — relay it and stop; it is not a parameter problem, so retrying changes nothing.
DEPLOY: Supports any project that can run as an HTTP service: static sites, small games, interactive content, and backend HTTP apps (Node.js, Python, Go). It probes the project structure, uploads the source code (compressed, excluding node_modules/.git/build output), installs dependencies inside a sandbox, starts the server on a single public port, and returns the access URL.
WHEN TO USE: the user asks to deploy / publish / go live / share an online link / preview in the cloud, OR the user @-mentions "sites". If the user did NOT explicitly ask to publish but the conversation is clearly about a deployable project they just changed, ASK the user first whether to publish/redeploy (in the same language as your reply) before calling this tool — do not silently deploy. Editing, styling or bug-fixing a page is a request for a local change, not a request to release it.
PRECONDITION — files must be written to disk FIRST: only call this tool AFTER all project files have been fully written to the target `directory` on disk. This tool compresses and uploads the directory as-is; if you call it before the code is saved (or while files are still being written), an empty or partial directory gets uploaded and the deployed site will be empty/broken. Finish writing every file (and confirm they exist under `directory`) before deploying. If you just edited files, make sure the edits are saved to disk first.
REQUIREMENTS for the project to be publishable (only a single public port is exposed, reached via a reverse-proxied domain):
(1) The service MUST listen on the PORT environment variable and bind 0.0.0.0.
(2) For a pure static site or a PDF/static asset, do NOT hand-roll a fragile Node server — serve it with Python: `python3 -m http.server "$PORT" --bind 0.0.0.0` (this is the default for static projects). When you CREATE a static site, do NOT add a package.json merely to declare "type": "module" for a local Node script — a static site does not need one. An existing empty package.json is TOLERATED and does not block publishing: the deployer detects it and falls back to static hosting successfully. So never ask the user to delete such a file, never delete it yourself, and never refuse to deploy because of it.
(3) For a Vite-based framework (Vite/Vue/React/Svelte), the dev/preview server MUST allow the reverse-proxy host: set `server.host = "0.0.0.0"` (or `--host 0.0.0.0`) and disable the host check by adding the deploy domain to `server.allowedHosts: true`, otherwise Vite rejects the request with "Blocked request. This host is not allowed."
If the target directory is EMPTY (nothing built yet), the tool returns dev guidance — develop the project first as a single-port HTTP service, then deploy again.
If the project genuinely CANNOT be exposed as an HTTP service (e.g. it is not a web/HTTP deliverable), do NOT force a deploy — tell the user that the current directory does not support publishing.
UNSUPPORTED PROJECTS — the tool checks before uploading anything: the sandbox serves Node.js / Python / Go / static sites on a single public HTTP port and provides NO database, cache or message queue. Java/Maven/Gradle projects and projects that need MySQL / PostgreSQL / Redis / MongoDB / Kafka are rejected without being uploaded, and the result is `{"type":"sites_deploy_unsupported"}` carrying a ready-to-use `userMessage`. The same rejection applies when the project has no runnable start entry (unsupportedKind `no-start-entry`): package.json declares no start / serve / dev script and no `main`, there is no server.js in the root directory, and the directory is not a static site that can be hosted as-is. A root-level server.js counts as a start entry (the npm `start` default runs `node server.js`). When that happens: relay `userMessage` to the user (translated into the reply language, keeping every point it makes) and STOP. Do NOT silently fall back to publishing a static placeholder / "project overview" page, do NOT retry with a different language/installCmd/startCmd to force it through, and do NOT strip the project's database on your own. If a downgrade or a rewrite might genuinely help, ASK the user first and wait for an explicit yes.
MINI PROGRAMS are supported and take their own path: when the directory (or one of its immediate subdirectories) is a mini program project — `project.config.json` / `app.wxss` / `app.acss` / `app.ttss`, or an `app.json` with a `pages` array — the tool creates the mini program application instead of publishing a web page, and returns `{"type":"sites_miniprogram_app_created"}` with a ready-to-use `userMessage` plus an `agentGuidance` that explains what is done and what the user still has to do. Relay the `userMessage` (translated into the reply language, keeping every point it makes), follow the `agentGuidance`, and STOP. Never substitute a web link for the mini program: do not write an HTML/H5 version and deploy that, do not deploy a sibling web page as if it fulfilled the request, and do not retry with a different `language` / `startCmd` to force a web publish — this path is decided by what is on disk.
NEVER A MINI PROGRAM BY ACCIDENT: apart from the mini-program path above, what this tool publishes is always a web page / HTTP service reached by a URL. A web publish can never be a mini program — that is a different codebase shipped through a different route. Set `miniProgramRequested: true` ONLY when the user actually asked for a mini program (小程序 / 微信小程序 / mini program / miniprogram) somewhere in this conversation AND what is being published is a web page. Doing so makes the result carry a ready-to-use `miniProgramNotice`, which you MUST then relay alongside the link — translated into the reply language, keeping every point it makes, never replaced by your own wording. Keep setting the flag and keep relaying the notice on re-publishes and content updates in the same conversation: someone who republishes still needs to know the new link is a web page, so do NOT drop it just because you already said it in an earlier turn. When the user never mentioned a mini program, leave the flag out and do NOT raise the topic at all — no notice, and no unsolicited remark that the link is "a web page, not a mini program". Never state or imply that a mini program was published.
RE-PUBLISHING OVERWRITES A LIVE LINK: publishing with an existing `appId` reuses that app's recorded sandbox when available, so the share link stays the same and whatever is currently online is replaced — already have shared with other people. Publishing consent does NOT carry over between turns: the user agreeing to publish earlier in this conversation does not authorize publishing again after you change the content. Set `userAskedToPublish: true` only when the user asked for it in their LATEST message (publish / deploy / go live / update the live site / 发布 / 上线 / 同步到线上, or an @-mention of sites). Otherwise the tool refuses to overwrite a live link and returns `{"type":"sites_deploy_needs_confirmation"}` carrying a ready-to-use `userMessage`: relay it, end your turn, and deploy only after the user agrees. Never set the flag just to get past that check.
WHICH APP GETS PUBLISHED IS DECIDED BY `directory`, NOT BY YOU: the tool looks up which app that directory belongs to and overwrites it, keeping its link and display name. With no recorded app yet, the first app is created automatically. You never need to ask the user which app to publish to, and you never need to pass `appId` for an ordinary publish — the destination is already determined, and passing an id can neither create a new app nor move the publish elsewhere. Publishing is ALWAYS an overwrite: there is no parameter for creating a second app alongside an existing one, so never promise the user a separate app and link — that is done from Settings - Data Management - Published Apps. Never infer a destination from directory names or app-name similarity. An app activated via the cloud service keeps its reserved domain and cloud-service Origin when published, so its cloud login keeps working.
domainPrefix IS REQUIRED for every deploy (action defaults to "deploy"). Generate it as a short English slug derived from the app name, not from the directory path: same idea as `appName`, but ASCII and short enough to be a domain segment — e.g. for an app about A-share sector rotation monitoring, domainPrefix="stock-rotation-dashboard". Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens only, no more than 32 characters. It requests a custom first segment for the app subdomain when a new app is created. It is NOT needed for action "unpublish" — leave it out there.
AFTER A SUCCESSFUL DEPLOY the result carries a `manageGuidance` instruction: follow it and tell the user where the app they just published can be managed. Do this on every successful deploy, re-publishes included.

